WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:06.750
No matter who you are, we all have somebody
in our lives that tells us competing is a

00:00:06.750 --> 00:00:08.130
terrible thing.

00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:09.910
It's not good to compete, honey.

00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:12.200
It's too much pressure when you compete, sweetheart.

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:14.740
It's not good to compete because someone's
got to lose.

00:00:14.740 --> 00:00:21.720
Anyone who has ever said that, that watches
sports, that watches T.V., that goes to restaurants

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:26.331
and posts reviews on Yelp, anybody who says
something like that yet does that, they're

00:00:26.331 --> 00:00:27.720
contradicting themselves.

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:29.020
Here's why.

00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:32.820
Everything great that ever happened stems
from competition.

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:34.030
Everything.

00:00:34.030 --> 00:00:35.920
World peace does not exist.

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:38.530
It just does not exist.

00:00:38.530 --> 00:00:42.409
Competition is one of the best things that
you see in business, sports, anything.

00:00:42.409 --> 00:00:48.930
And by the way, typically those who make it
to the highest ranks in sports, in business,

00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:53.350
in life, in entrepreneurship, in anything,
they're typically the most competitive people

00:00:53.350 --> 00:00:54.929
you can ever meet in your life.

00:00:54.929 --> 00:00:58.229
So today I'm going to talk to you about 10
Reasons Why To Compete.

00:00:58.229 --> 00:01:01.579
Not 10 benefits of competing, 10 reasons why
to compete.

00:01:01.579 --> 00:01:03.479
I'll go through them fairly quickly.

00:01:03.479 --> 00:01:06.420
#1: I think competition brings out the best
in you.

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:11.190
When you're competing, especially if you go
against somebody that is 10 times better than

00:01:11.190 --> 00:01:15.550
you, you all of a sudden like, "wait a minute,
I'm not as good as I thought I was.

00:01:15.550 --> 00:01:16.860
I have to learn to fight.

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:20.050
I'm going to have to learn to pick my game
up.

00:01:20.050 --> 00:01:22.860
I cannot believe how much faster he is than
me.

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:25.850
I cannot believe how better they are at customer
service than us.

00:01:25.850 --> 00:01:28.980
I cannot believe how amazing they are with
their systems.

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:32.920
How are we going to compete with these guys?

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:34.720
We've got to figure out a way to get better."

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:37.700
So it brings out the best in you when you
compete.

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:40.980
#2: The pain of losing is a great motivator.

00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:45.260
The pain of losing is one of the greatest
motivators of a competitor.

00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:47.210
Let me explain something to you.

00:01:47.210 --> 00:01:52.610
The same people that say competition is not
good for you, I guarantee you if you sit down

00:01:52.610 --> 00:01:55.940
and have a private conversation with them
and they're willing to be open, there was

00:01:55.940 --> 00:02:00.240
a very painful loss at some point of their
lives.

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:01.240
What could it be?

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:03.620
They lost the girl to another man.

00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:05.590
They lost the man to another girl.

00:02:05.590 --> 00:02:06.860
That hurt them.

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:09.110
They lost a customer to somebody.

00:02:09.110 --> 00:02:10.539
They lost a game.

00:02:10.539 --> 00:02:15.920
Someone beat them in a public event, at a
sporting event, and they didn't like that.

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:18.799
They lost in a debate, publicly.

00:02:18.799 --> 00:02:24.900
They completely stumbled once and instead
of getting up and wanting to compete again,

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:29.840
which we've all had our embarrassing moments
in our lives, they chose to let that define

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:30.840
them.

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:33.659
Those chose to let that define them.

00:02:33.659 --> 00:02:37.420
Anybody that competes at the highest level,
they had a very painful loss.

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:39.389
Very painful loss.

00:02:39.389 --> 00:02:45.810
I remember, I can tell you, I've won in business
for the last 15 years I've won many, many

00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:50.109
times, but I can tell you, the ones I remember
vividly are my losses.

00:02:50.109 --> 00:02:54.889
And the ones that got me to step up, look
at myself in the mirror and dust myself off

00:02:54.889 --> 00:02:59.190
and say, "Man, that hurt," that's what caused
me to compete and get better, and improve

00:02:59.190 --> 00:03:01.599
my business to the next level.

00:03:01.599 --> 00:03:03.029
#3: Competition keeps you young.

00:03:03.029 --> 00:03:06.569
Believe it or not, those who are engaged in
the game, they're younger, because they're

00:03:06.569 --> 00:03:08.499
not rusting out.

00:03:08.499 --> 00:03:10.069
Man, what if I burn out?

00:03:10.069 --> 00:03:13.550
I would definitely rather burn out than rust
out.

00:03:13.550 --> 00:03:18.370
I'd definitely rather be in the game and like,
man, let's go let's go than sit around and

00:03:18.370 --> 00:03:23.129
just watch everything just rust out and not
be engaged in competing.

00:03:23.129 --> 00:03:25.739
I would rather see that happen any day of
the week.

00:03:25.739 --> 00:03:26.969
So competition keeps you young.

00:03:26.969 --> 00:03:30.069
You've got to stay young because in order
to keep up with everybody else, you are kind

00:03:30.069 --> 00:03:32.050
of going to end up staying young.

00:03:32.050 --> 00:03:34.549
#4: It distracts you from problems in your
life.

00:03:34.549 --> 00:03:40.279
If you think about the last time you really
delved into problems so deep that it almost

00:03:40.279 --> 00:03:48.559
drove you nuts, I can tell you at least for
myself, any time I go so deep into a problem

00:03:48.559 --> 00:03:54.439
that it makes me think what if. . . it's when
I have so much time on my hands and I'm not

00:03:54.439 --> 00:03:56.400
in the hunt for something.

00:03:56.400 --> 00:04:00.260
Because when you're in the hunt for something,
when you're competing for something, when

00:04:00.260 --> 00:04:03.620
you're improving to do something, you don't
have time to think about problems.

00:04:03.620 --> 00:04:07.829
You actually have a positive distraction.

00:04:07.829 --> 00:04:10.019
Everyone in life has distractions.

00:04:10.019 --> 00:04:11.019
Everybody.

00:04:11.019 --> 00:04:14.159
A kid could be a positive distraction to a
business.

00:04:14.159 --> 00:04:15.409
A business could be a positive distraction
to a business.

00:04:15.409 --> 00:04:18.239
A business could be a positive distraction
to an ex-girlfriend.

00:04:18.239 --> 00:04:22.820
There are a lot of positive distractions.

00:04:22.820 --> 00:04:28.270
Competition is a positive distraction and
it takes you into the game so much that you

00:04:28.270 --> 00:04:32.490
don't have time to be thinking about all the
problems and possibilities of the world coming

00:04:32.490 --> 00:04:33.490
to an end.

00:04:33.490 --> 00:04:37.730
You don't have time for that, because your
engaged, you're in the arena competing.

00:04:37.730 --> 00:04:41.160
#5: Believe it or not, when you compete, you
build new relationships.

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:42.850
And somebody may say, "Wait a minute.

00:04:42.850 --> 00:04:44.460
When you compete you build new relationships?"

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:45.460
Yes!

00:04:45.460 --> 00:04:47.170
Let me explain it to you in the simplest way.

00:04:47.170 --> 00:04:49.540
Let's just say you're a kid that plays tennis.

00:04:49.540 --> 00:04:50.540
Hypothetically.

00:04:50.540 --> 00:04:53.100
So you start playing tennis with your dad.

00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:55.550
And you're pretty good.

00:04:55.550 --> 00:04:56.990
You play with your dad and you're actually
pretty good.

00:04:56.990 --> 00:04:58.650
Then you start playing with your brother and
you're actually good.

00:04:58.650 --> 00:04:59.650
You beat your brother.

00:04:59.650 --> 00:05:00.800
And that's your older brother by two years.

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:02.510
And you beat him in tennis.

00:05:02.510 --> 00:05:03.510
Okay, cool.

00:05:03.510 --> 00:05:05.270
So everybody so far you know is who?

00:05:05.270 --> 00:05:06.270
Family.

00:05:06.270 --> 00:05:09.661
Then, you're dad and your mom say, "Why don't
we put you in a different place where you

00:05:09.661 --> 00:05:11.530
start competing against some coaches."

00:05:11.530 --> 00:05:17.440
And you start getting coached and your backhand
is good, your serve is good, your footwork

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:20.480
is getting good, then the coach says, "You
know what, I think we should put her in a

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:21.480
league."

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:22.480
And then you go into a league.

00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:23.480
You're not in high school.

00:05:23.480 --> 00:05:24.480
You're 12 or 13 years old.

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:27.670
You go into a league and in the league you
start doing good.

00:05:27.670 --> 00:05:30.470
And then all of a sudden you beat a couple
of guys.

00:05:30.470 --> 00:05:33.370
And then you get beat once by somebody that's
very good.

00:05:33.370 --> 00:05:34.790
She is 10 times better than you.

00:05:34.790 --> 00:05:35.790
And oh my gosh.

00:05:35.790 --> 00:05:40.990
And the next thing you know, you win one,
where you go to a tournament, and there's

00:05:40.990 --> 00:05:43.110
30 girls that are now competing.

00:05:43.110 --> 00:05:47.060
You officially build new relationships with
30 new competitors.

00:05:47.060 --> 00:05:48.270
And you're one of them.

00:05:48.270 --> 00:05:50.000
Now you have new relationships.

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:54.330
If you're in the business world, and say you're
a social media consultant.

00:05:54.330 --> 00:05:56.000
You have to go to all the social media conventions.

00:05:56.000 --> 00:06:00.000
You have to go to all the social media events
to find out what all the up and coming things

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:01.000
are.

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:02.000
If you don't go to them, you're being left
behind.

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:03.000
You're not a competitor.

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:04.030
What if you don't do it?

00:06:04.030 --> 00:06:07.500
If you don't do it, you know nothing about
Snapchat, you know nothing about all this

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:09.110
stuff that's changed, you don't know about
it.

00:06:09.110 --> 00:06:10.350
So you're not a competitor.

00:06:10.350 --> 00:06:11.350
You've being left behind.

00:06:11.350 --> 00:06:13.760
No one's going to hire you because you don't
know what's up to date because you're not

00:06:13.760 --> 00:06:14.760
a competitor.

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:17.310
But if you go to all these functions all of
a sudden people are going to know that you

00:06:17.310 --> 00:06:21.120
know what you're talking about, now you're
part of a committee of 10 other competitors

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:24.500
that are fighting to get the same customers
you're going for, but you're learning how

00:06:24.500 --> 00:06:27.080
to get better because you're among these other
competitors.

00:06:27.080 --> 00:06:29.810
You build a lot of relationships if you do
compete.

00:06:29.810 --> 00:06:35.100
#6: I was at a convention this past week in
Palm Springs.

00:06:35.100 --> 00:06:36.100
Had a great time.

00:06:36.100 --> 00:06:37.210
There was a lot of energy.

00:06:37.210 --> 00:06:40.800
This place had 1300 1400 people at this convention
in Palm Springs.

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:43.110
And I watch everybody.

00:06:43.110 --> 00:06:44.450
I watch everybody.

00:06:44.450 --> 00:06:49.370
And one of the things that I pay very close
attention to, there are those that are established,

00:06:49.370 --> 00:06:51.080
let's just say in business.

00:06:51.080 --> 00:06:55.530
But then there are those that are young and
hungry and coming up in business.

00:06:55.530 --> 00:06:58.020
There's not age, they're young in business.

00:06:58.020 --> 00:06:59.020
They're not necessarily young.

00:06:59.020 --> 00:07:00.020
They could be 42 years old, but they're young
in business.

00:07:00.020 --> 00:07:04.770
And you see the look in their eyes, this competitor.

00:07:04.770 --> 00:07:07.680
And you see someone that's established, their
eyes are a little droopy.

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:09.490
They've lost the fire.

00:07:09.490 --> 00:07:13.200
And this other person's coming up and they're
trying to do whatever they can, but they keep

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:16.100
losing, keep losing, keep losing, but they're
not giving up.

00:07:16.100 --> 00:07:17.660
Let me tell you something.

00:07:17.660 --> 00:07:20.870
These guys earn a lot of respect.

00:07:20.870 --> 00:07:22.880
They get respect from everybody.

00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:26.450
Because it's kind of like in school when you
fight somebody, and you beat this person up

00:07:26.450 --> 00:07:28.430
but this person swings back every time you
fight them.

00:07:28.430 --> 00:07:32.120
Even if you beat them, you don't want to fight
this person next time because listen, this

00:07:32.120 --> 00:07:34.390
other person I fight, he never swings.

00:07:34.390 --> 00:07:38.250
This person I fight he won't stop swinging
until I kick his butt and he typically gets

00:07:38.250 --> 00:07:39.250
me a couple of times.

00:07:39.250 --> 00:07:40.750
I don't want to fight this guy.

00:07:40.750 --> 00:07:41.750
Gain respect.

00:07:41.750 --> 00:07:43.180
Even though this person lost.

00:07:43.180 --> 00:07:45.490
There's a lot of respect for a competitor.

00:07:45.490 --> 00:07:49.010
There's a lot of respect for somebody that
goes to the last minute and fights to the

00:07:49.010 --> 00:07:51.120
very last minute - even if they lose.

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:55.170
In sports you hear an athlete saying, "You
know, these guys were tough people we faced.

00:07:55.170 --> 00:07:57.030
This was not an easy one.

00:07:57.030 --> 00:07:59.090
They fought to the very last minute."

00:07:59.090 --> 00:08:02.500
And you'll see sometimes where there's oh,
these guys gave up after their first game

00:08:02.500 --> 00:08:04.360
because they lost by 28 points, that's no
respect.

00:08:04.360 --> 00:08:07.620
You don't gain respect with that.

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:15.120
#7: When you're a competitor, one of the reasons
when you compete, your brain gets wired in

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:16.810
improvement mode.

00:08:16.810 --> 00:08:18.920
Your brain gets wired in improvement mode.

00:08:18.920 --> 00:08:22.940
Let's just say you run a real estate business
and you lost one of your clients and they

00:08:22.940 --> 00:08:25.060
went and put their listing with another guy.

00:08:25.060 --> 00:08:26.060
Why?

00:08:26.060 --> 00:08:30.190
And you found out the reason they went with
this other guy is because that guy has five

00:08:30.190 --> 00:08:36.050
people that are passing out flyers and that
guy has got people that are doing all these

00:08:36.050 --> 00:08:39.840
other marketing for him and he's got five
different websites and he's on Craig's List

00:08:39.840 --> 00:08:41.070
and you didn't do that.

00:08:41.070 --> 00:08:42.070
Well guess what?

00:08:42.070 --> 00:08:45.270
If you want to compete with this guy, you
have to not lose future listings, you better

00:08:45.270 --> 00:08:46.270
improve.

00:08:46.270 --> 00:08:48.750
Don't get out of the real estate business
- you're going to lose to him every single

00:08:48.750 --> 00:08:49.750
time.

00:08:49.750 --> 00:08:52.770
If you run a coffee shop, and there's a coffee
shop across the street, customers used to

00:08:52.770 --> 00:08:54.510
come to you, now they're no longer coming
to you.

00:08:54.510 --> 00:08:55.510
They're going across the street.

00:08:55.510 --> 00:08:58.630
That customer kept telling you, hey, with
this coffee, why don't you have some sweets

00:08:58.630 --> 00:08:59.630
for us have.

00:08:59.630 --> 00:09:01.030
And you say, "No, our specialty is mainly
coffee."

00:09:01.030 --> 00:09:03.020
Now you lost that business that's going across
the street.

00:09:03.020 --> 00:09:05.279
Now you may want to consider having some kind
of sweets.

00:09:05.279 --> 00:09:08.050
You know, hey, can you put some chairs outside
so we can sit outside?

00:09:08.050 --> 00:09:09.050
I don't want to sit inside.

00:09:09.050 --> 00:09:10.840
The weather's sometimes good, I just want
to sit outside.

00:09:10.840 --> 00:09:12.520
"No, we're only an inside cafe."

00:09:12.520 --> 00:09:15.380
You've got to figure out a way to go a little
bit outside, because you've got to figure

00:09:15.380 --> 00:09:18.510
out ways to improve otherwise you're going
to lose customers to the guys across the street.

00:09:18.510 --> 00:09:23.130
When you are a competitor, you're constantly
going to be in the mode of improvement, because

00:09:23.130 --> 00:09:24.130
somebody's constantly going to be beating
you.

00:09:24.130 --> 00:09:28.220
And by the way, to those of you that are watching
this that are not choosing to be competitive

00:09:28.220 --> 00:09:29.430
- guess what?

00:09:29.430 --> 00:09:33.370
When businesses compete, it serves you and
I.

00:09:33.370 --> 00:09:36.520
When restaurants compete, it serves you and
I.

00:09:36.520 --> 00:09:39.080
When movie theaters compete, it serves you
and I.

00:09:39.080 --> 00:09:41.890
When universities compete, it serves you and
I.

00:09:41.890 --> 00:09:42.890
Because they're trying to make it better.

00:09:42.890 --> 00:09:46.350
I was at a USC meeting with the president
of USC.

00:09:46.350 --> 00:09:48.430
He invited 13 different people at this table.

00:09:48.430 --> 00:09:51.529
We were sitting there having lunch together
and he starts talking about how they're goal

00:09:51.529 --> 00:09:53.080
is to raise 6 billion dollars.

00:09:53.080 --> 00:09:55.450
And we asked, "What are you raising 6 billion
dollars for?

00:09:55.450 --> 00:09:58.280
He said, "We're building a cafeteria.

00:09:58.280 --> 00:10:04.640
It's a 1.5 billion dollar project and we're
building a cafeteria identical from the Harry

00:10:04.640 --> 00:10:05.750
Potter movie.

00:10:05.750 --> 00:10:06.930
No way.

00:10:06.930 --> 00:10:07.930
Yes.

00:10:07.930 --> 00:10:08.930
Why?

00:10:08.930 --> 00:10:11.670
Because they want their kids to want to go
to USC, because these kids are accustomed

00:10:11.670 --> 00:10:15.430
to watching Harry Potter, they want to be
able to say, "our cafeteria's cool" -- because

00:10:15.430 --> 00:10:19.580
they're listening, because the one school
he mentioned the entire time we were sitting

00:10:19.580 --> 00:10:20.690
there was who?

00:10:20.690 --> 00:10:21.690
Harvard.

00:10:21.690 --> 00:10:22.850
USC wants to compete with Harvard.

00:10:22.850 --> 00:10:23.850
Think about that.

00:10:23.850 --> 00:10:24.850
That's impressive.

00:10:24.850 --> 00:10:28.550
If I got kids that want to go to a school
and I know a guy like that is competing against

00:10:28.550 --> 00:10:30.270
Harvard, I'm interested in that.

00:10:30.270 --> 00:10:33.320
Because they want to make the school better
for our kids.

00:10:33.320 --> 00:10:34.320
Competition.

00:10:34.320 --> 00:10:36.270
#8: Fulfillment.

00:10:36.270 --> 00:10:38.530
There's a lot of excitement and fulfillment
when you compete.

00:10:38.530 --> 00:10:40.660
There's a certain excitement you get when
you compete.

00:10:40.660 --> 00:10:43.339
I can't describe it to you.

00:10:43.339 --> 00:10:47.260
When people are in the arena competing, there's
a certain sense of fulfillment.

00:10:47.260 --> 00:10:50.450
There's a lot of people right now saying,
"Did you see that picture with Michael Phelps,

00:10:50.450 --> 00:10:52.300
he's swimming and the other guy's looking
at him?

00:10:52.300 --> 00:10:53.780
Man, that guy's a loser.

00:10:53.780 --> 00:10:56.050
That's why you focus on winners."

00:10:56.050 --> 00:10:58.320
I'm sorry, he's in the Olympics.

00:10:58.320 --> 00:11:00.589
He made it to the Olympics.

00:11:00.589 --> 00:11:01.750
That's pretty epic.

00:11:01.750 --> 00:11:03.640
I shared the picture myself.

00:11:03.640 --> 00:11:05.470
But that guy's in the Olympics.

00:11:05.470 --> 00:11:07.870
He's fulfilled, he's going to say, "I was
an Olympian."

00:11:07.870 --> 00:11:13.090
Now obviously there's a very small community
of great ones, but at least he went and he

00:11:13.090 --> 00:11:15.720
made it to compete with the great one.

00:11:15.720 --> 00:11:18.000
Even though the world is going to say he lost.

00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:19.510
To his family, he's an Olympian.

00:11:19.510 --> 00:11:24.580
For his kids, their always going to say, "Daddy
was an Olympian."

00:11:24.580 --> 00:11:27.670
The girl, the gymnast that lost to the other
girl that was an absolute beat by two points,

